Required Notice: Copyright (c) 2026 Woojin Go (https://github.com/wjgoarxiv/agentvfx)

The agentvfx licence has two parts that apply together: the Additional Terms in Part 1, and the licence text in Part 2,
which is the PolyForm Noncommercial License 1.0.0 reproduced unchanged. Part 1 adds a permission, a credit condition
and a restriction to Part 2; it does not change any wording of Part 2. "These terms" in Part 2 means Parts 1 and 2
together, so everything granted in Part 1 is one of "your licenses" and is subject to Acceptance, Patent Defense and
Violations. To meet "Notices" in Part 2, give each recipient of the software this whole file, or its URL
(https://github.com/wjgoarxiv/agentvfx/blob/main/LICENSE); the polyformproject.org URL alone is not enough.

## Part 1. Additional Terms

### 1. Creator permission

If you are an individual (a natural person), you may run the software to render video, images and audio ("output"),
and use that output in your own creative work for any purpose, including commercial purposes. For example, you may
release your own music video, earn advertising, subscription, sponsorship or tip income on your own channel, or play
the output in your own live or VJ performance.

For this permission:

- you count as an individual if you are a sole proprietor (for example a registered 개인사업자) or self-employed,
  as long as the work is your own;
- several individuals making a work together under their own names, such as a band, count as individuals together,
  as long as none of them acts for a company or other organisation in doing so;
- sponsored content on your own channel or in your own work is covered.

This permission does not cover:

- work for an employer, or on behalf of a company or other organisation (a noncommercial organisation keeps only the
  permissions it already has in Part 2);
- output made for someone else to publish or use as theirs, such as a client's advertisement or a deliverable for a
  label or a brand;
- selling, licensing or offering the software, its templates or presets, or rendering as a service, or offering output
  on its own as a product (stock footage, loops, packs, NFTs); output may be sold only as part of a larger work of
  your own.

Any other commercial use needs a commercial licence from the licensor. Ask at
https://github.com/wjgoarxiv/agentvfx/issues.

### 2. No AI training

Under any permission, you may not use the software, its source, shaders, templates or presets to train, fine-tune or
evaluate a machine-learning model, or to build a dataset for that purpose. Using an AI agent to operate the software
for your own use is not training and is allowed.

### 3. Output

As far as the law allows, you own the output you make. The licensor claims no rights in your output except in any part
of the software or templates that the output contains. People who only watch, host, stream or receive output need no
licence from the licensor, and they are not bound by these terms. You are, and you remain responsible for what you
publish or hand over.

### 4. Credit

If you publish, stream, publicly show or give anyone output made with agentvfx, under any permission, including
Part 2, you must credit agentvfx.

By default every render burns a small "agentvfx" credit into a corner of the frame. While that credit is visible in
what you publish, you need nothing more; do not crop, cover or edit it out.

If you switch it off in the score (`"credit": { "overlay": false }`), or the output carries no on-screen credit (for
example a web export), you must instead credit agentvfx in text where viewers can reasonably find it (the description,
caption, credits, or a page the output links to):

    Made with agentvfx by Woojin Go - https://github.com/wjgoarxiv/agentvfx

Where space is short, `VFX: agentvfx (github.com/wjgoarxiv/agentvfx)` is enough. Every render writes the text credit
to CREDIT.txt next to the film and into the MP4 metadata; leave both in place.

When you give output to someone who will publish it, pass the credit on with it: leave the on-screen credit in the
frame, or give them the text line and CREDIT.txt. The credit is a condition of every licence under these terms. A
missing credit can be added within the period that "Violations" in Part 2 allows.

### 5. Web export

If you use scripts/export-web.mjs, keep the LICENSE file it writes with the exported folder.

### 6. Names

These terms give no right to use the name "agentvfx", the name "Woojin Go" or the agentvfx logo, except to give the
credit in section 4 and to say truthfully where a work comes from. If you share a changed copy of the software (a fork),
you must give it a name that does not contain "agentvfx", you must not present it as agentvfx or as made or endorsed by
the licensor, and you must say that it is changed from agentvfx, with a link to https://github.com/wjgoarxiv/agentvfx.

### 7. Earlier copies and third-party portions

Copies obtained under the MIT License before 5 November 2026 keep that licence for those copies only. Every version
this repository publishes is under these terms. Portions adapted from mexicat/pdoom-video remain under their own
MIT License, and the capture engine copied from lit-typographic-motion is MIT in its own repository; see
skills/agentvfx/NOTICE for the files and the notice.

